# ==========================================================================
# THIS FILE IS A REFERENCE, NOT A SWITCH.
# Nila does NOT read a `.env` file: nothing calls a dotenv loader, on purpose
# (a stray `.env` in a project folder is exactly what an agent should ignore).
# Export these in your shell, or set them as Windows user variables. Anything
# you set inside `.env` and nowhere else has no effect.
# ==========================================================================
#
# IMPORTANT: almost nothing here is required, and most settings are NOT
# environment variables at all. Nila reads its configuration from the vault's
# config file via `brain config set <key> <value>`. Only a handful of real
# environment variables exist; they are listed under "Real variables" below.
# Setting a name that is not listed there does nothing.

# ============================================================================
# Real environment variables (these are actually read by the code)
# ============================================================================

# Vault location. Read by: brain/config.py
# Default: ~/.personal-brain (on Windows the .bat files set
# %LOCALAPPDATA%\Nila\vault so your data sits outside the program folder).
# BRAIN_HOME=/home/you/.personal-brain

# Print full tracebacks instead of "HALTED. ..." when a command fails.
# Read by: brain/cli/main.py
# BRAIN_DEBUG=1

# Bearer token for the e2ee sync server. This is the DEFAULT name that
# `sync.token_env` resolves to; you can point that key at any variable you like.
# Read by: brain/cloud/protocol.py
# BRAIN_SYNC_TOKEN=

# Bearer token for the UPDATE server (the server side of the update channel).
# This is the default for `cloud/update_server/app.py --token-env`.
# Read by: cloud/update_server/app.py
# BRAIN_UPDATE_TOKEN=

# API keys: the PREFERRED route is the sealed vault, not an environment variable.
#   python brain.py connectors key gemini        # hidden prompt, stored sealed
#   python brain.py connectors test gemini       # one small fixed call, counted
# The variables below still work as a fallback, and are the DEFAULT names that
# `agent.api_key_env` / `agent.online_key_env` resolve to, per provider type.
# Read by: brain/agent/providers.py
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Integration secrets. These names are NOT fixed - the gateway resolves whatever
# you declare as {"$env": "NAME"} in an integration config, so pick any name.
# The two below are illustrative, matching config/integrations.example.json.
# A literal secret written into a config file is rejected by design.
# NOTION_TOKEN=
# GITHUB_TOKEN=

# ============================================================================
# Everything else is config, not environment. Use `brain config set`.
# ============================================================================

# --- model provider ----------------------------------------------------------
#   brain config set agent.provider openai      # openai|anthropic|ollama|local
#   brain config set agent.model gpt-4o-mini
#   brain config set agent.base_url https://api.openai.com/v1
#   brain config set agent.api_key_env OPENAI_API_KEY
#   brain config set agent.temperature 0.2
#
# Ollama defaults to http://127.0.0.1:11434 and model llama3.2:
#   brain config set agent.provider ollama
#   brain config set agent.base_url http://127.0.0.1:11434
#   brain config set agent.model llama3.1
#
# With no provider configured, Nila runs the offline rule-based LocalBrain.

# --- e2ee sync ---------------------------------------------------------------
# All THREE keys are required. `sync.backend` defaults to "local", which writes
# blobs to a directory on disk instead of contacting any server - so if you set
# only the url and token, sync will appear to succeed while nothing is uploaded.
#   brain config set sync.backend http
#   brain config set sync.url http://127.0.0.1:8790
#   brain config set sync.token_env BRAIN_SYNC_TOKEN
# Verify it really reached the server:
#   curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $BRAIN_SYNC_TOKEN" http://127.0.0.1:8790/health
# `keys` should be non-zero after a push.

# --- egress policy -----------------------------------------------------------
#   brain config set agent.allow_cloud_context false   # default: false
# Even when true, `restricted` data NEVER leaves the device. There is no flag
# that permits it. See docs/THREAT_MODEL.md boundary B7.

# --- signed updates ----------------------------------------------------------
# Update channels are registered with the CLI, not the environment:
#   brain update-channel-add stable https://updates.example.internal/manifest.json
# Signature mode is per channel: strict | tofu | insecure.
